<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Plain HTTP has to be allowed, and it is worth being explicit about why rather than
  leaving a future reader to assume it was carelessness.

  The server address is typed in by the user (ServerStore), and in this business it is
  normally a machine on the workshop network — `192.168.1.10:8080` during the parallel run,
  a laptop in the yard, the office server before anyone puts a certificate on it. None of
  those have TLS, and Android has refused cleartext by default since API 28, so without
  this every LAN address fails with an IOException that reads like the server is down.

  Android's network security config cannot express "private address ranges only" — it
  matches domains, not CIDR blocks — so this cannot be narrowed declaratively. The
  narrowing happens in the UI instead: the setup screen warns when the address is http://
  and not a private address, which is the case where cleartext actually costs something.

  What would remove this: putting a certificate on the production server and having the
  fitters type https://. Then this file becomes a debug-only override.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
